---
// The design system, as a page of the site itself: principles, colours (drawn
// from the real tokens), type, shape, marks and the posts kit. Dev only: it is
// the reference while the redesign is built, not part of the site.
import { homePosts, workSince } from "../components/home/window/data";
import StatArrow from "../components/site/StatArrow.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect("/");

// The home window sample below is drawn from the real posts, as the window
// is, so its rows and counts are never a post behind: the newest post, and
// the next one whose result went from an old value to a new one.
const posts = await homePosts();
const since = await workSince();
const first = posts[0];
const second = posts.slice(1).find((p) => p.before) ?? posts[1];
const rows = [first, second].filter((p) => p !== undefined);
---

<Base
  title="Design system · Colin Cheung"
  description="The design system for colincheung.dev."
>
  <div class="ds">
    <div class="top">
      <span>colincheung.dev · design system</span>
      <span class="f">the theme button is in the nav</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Grey does the structure. The accent points.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The one reference for how the site looks, drawn with the site's own tokens
      and fonts. Dark is the default; light is a soft toggle. The colours come
      from <code>docs/redesign/comps/tokens.mts</code>; when a decision changes,
      this page changes in the same commit. What was tried and dropped lives in
      <code>docs/design-rejected.md</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Principles</h2>
    <p>What every decision comes back to.</p>
    <ol class="princ">
      <li>
        <b>One thing per glance.</b>
        The name leads; one thing keeps moving on the first screen; one accent,
        used sparingly. The home page's first screen is the one place three
        purple things share a glance: the hero's highlight, the window's focus
        and the favicon's bar (DESIGN.md, the Accent Points Rule's exception).
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Real over decorative.</b>
        Real numbers, live weather, real posts and roles. A slogan or a
        placeholder figure gets cut.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Personality in the content, not the chrome.</b>
        The one-liners, 張志權 and the homelab carry the character; surfaces
        stay grey and flat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Light type.</b>
        Thin for the voice, semibold only for names. Anything that reads thick
        gets lighter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The terminal shows, it does not ask.</b>
        One window of panels with real readouts; nothing to type. Hover and a
        few keys only choose what to look at.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A post's result is said in one place.</b>
        On the home page that is the writing panel: its row, and the readout
        under the list while it is selected. No other panel or section repeats
        it. The window's readouts do restate what their own list or figure
        shows, on purpose, and the role is in the hero and in fastfetch.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Motion has a cause.</b>
        Slow, pauses on hover, off with reduced motion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Fewer options.</b>
        Once a choice is made the comp loses the switch, and the dropped idea
        goes in the rejected list. For the home window (Nov 2026) Colin asked to
        be shown variations and picked from them; that every change of that size
        should be shown that way is inferred from the one request, so ask him.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Colour</h2>
    <p>
      Three kinds of colour and nothing else: one grey scale, one purple
      spectrum, three status colours. Each is one OKLCH value; only lightness
      changes between light and dark, so dark mode is the same palette, not a
      second one. One bounded exception, the small print of the home window, is
      drawn further down.
    </p>
    <div class="scale">
      <h3>neutral · hue 255, barely tinted cool · 12 steps</h3>
      <div class="sw" data-sw="n"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="scale">
      <h3>
        accent · purple spectrum, hue 308, 6 even steps from dark up to the
        accent, the same in both themes · each job is one step
      </h3>
      <div class="sw six" data-sw="a"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="scale">
      <h3>
        status · green ok, amber warn, red danger · steps 3 (wash), 9 (dot), 11
        (text) only
      </h3>
      <div class="sw three" data-sw="s"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>surfaces</h3>
        <p>dark (the default): page n1 · panel n3 · well n4</p>
        <p>
          light (the toggle): page n5 · panel n3 · figures and quotes n2 · well
          n4, no pure white
        </p>
        <p class="m">separated by tone, never by a shadow</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>text</h3>
        <p>ink = n12 · for titles and values</p>
        <p class="m">muted = n11 · the line under a title</p>
        <p class="f">faint = n10 · dates, labels, captions (4.5:1)</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>edges</h3>
        <p>line = ink at 10% · strong = 16% · faint = 5%</p>
        <p class="m">dark: white at the same alphas</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>accent · results and links</h3>
        <p class="stat">
          <span class="was">12 h <StatArrow /></span><b>11 min</b>
          <span class="f">the result</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/blog/">a link to the writing</a>
          <span class="f">where it goes</span>
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          plus the one highlight in a hero drawing, and a control under the
          pointer. Never chrome (prompts, carets, window dots, the nav,
          separators), a surface, a heading or a button. It also stands for
          "info": there is no separate blue status colour.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>status · traffic lights, small</h3>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:0">
          <span class="badge badge--ok"><i></i>live</span
          ><span class="badge badge--warn"><i></i>draft</span
          ><span class="badge badge--danger"><i></i>down</span>
        </div>
        <p class="f" style="margin-top:.6rem">
          dots and small badges only; always with a word, never colour alone
          (amber fills are under 3:1 on white everywhere). Red would appear only
          for a real failure; a result that got worse stays ink. None is in the
          site's chrome today: the home window says status in words.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>controls are ink</h3>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:0">
          <a class="btn btn--ink" href="/blog/jest-to-vitest/">Read the post</a
          ><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/cv/">CV</a
          ><span class="ico"
            ><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path
                d="M12 .5C5.7.5.5 5.7.5 12c0 5.1 3.3 9.4 7.9 10.9.6.1.8-.2.8-.6v-2c-3.2.7-3.9-1.4-3.9-1.4-.5-1.3-1.3-1.7-1.3-1.7-1-.7.1-.7.1-.7 1.2.1 1.8 1.2 1.8 1.2 1 1.8 2.7 1.3 3.4 1 .1-.8.4-1.3.7-1.6-2.6-.3-5.3-1.3-5.3-5.7 0-1.3.5-2.3 1.2-3.1-.1-.3-.5-1.5.1-3.1 0 0 1-.3 3.2 1.2a11 11 0 0 1 5.8 0c2.2-1.5 3.2-1.2 3.2-1.2.6 1.6.2 2.8.1 3.1.8.8 1.2 1.8 1.2 3.1 0 4.4-2.7 5.4-5.3 5.7.4.4.8 1.1.8 2.2v3.2c0 .3.2.7.8.6 4.6-1.5 7.9-5.8 7.9-10.9C23.5 5.7 18.3.5 12 .5z"
              />
            </svg></span
          >
        </div>
        <p class="f" style="margin-top:.6rem">
          black or outlined, 6px corners; no blue buttons, no pills
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Type</h2>
    <p>
      Sora for names and the voice, Geist for reading, DM Mono for data.
      Tracking tightens as size grows.
    </p>
    <div class="card type">
      <p class="t-name">Colin Cheung</p>
      <p class="t-mono">
        Sora 600 · −0.05em · the name, and section headings at 1.3rem
      </p>
      <p class="t-line" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        still waiting to reverse a <b>linked list</b> at work
      </p>
      <p class="t-mono">
        Sora 200, punchline 400 · −0.03em · the one-liners and the worked-at
        names
      </p>
      <p class="t-title" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        Part 3: How Jev performs on Aliens
      </p>
      <p class="t-mono">
        Geist 400 · post and list titles; never 500 or heavier in lists
      </p>
      <p class="t-body" style="margin-top:1rem">
        Body at 16–17px, line-height 1.55, muted when it sits under a title.
      </p>
      <p class="t-mono">Geist 400</p>
      <p class="t-mono" style="margin-top:1rem;color:var(--ink)">
        fastfetch · 4.5 GB → 221 MB · 27 Oct
      </p>
      <p class="t-mono">
        DM Mono 300–500 · the home window (400 and 500 only), dates, stats,
        captions
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Shape and depth</h2>
    <p>
      Small, consistent corners and no shadows. Depth comes from tone: a white
      panel on a grey page, a darker well inside a panel.
    </p>
    <div class="shape">
      <div style="width:12rem;height:6rem;border-radius:var(--r)">
        8px<br>panels, cards, figures
      </div>
      <div
        style="width:8rem;height:2.6rem;border-radius:var(--r-ctl);border:1px solid var(--line-strong)"
      >
        6px · controls
      </div>
      <div
        style="width:5rem;height:1.6rem;border-radius:var(--r-chip);background:var(--well)"
      >
        4px · chips
      </div>
      <div
        style="width:1.6rem;height:1.6rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--n6)"
        title="only dots are round"
      ></div>
    </div>
    <p class="f" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      Only dots are round. No pills, no 12px+ corners, no drop shadows, no
      glows; the one halo is the focus ring on the current dot. No gradient as a
      surface or a decoration: the uses that stand are a fade where content runs
      under an edge (rows under the phone's pinned readout, the ends of the
      worked-at marquee), the radar's sweep, and hard-stop patterns that draw a
      thing (the tunnel's dashes, hatching). No borders on panels either, with
      one exception: the home window, below.
    </p>

    <h2>Marks and motion</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>the slash</h3>
        <p>
          PodFather <span class="t-mono">2017</span
          ><span class="slash">/</span>Skyscanner
          <span class="t-mono">2020</span>
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          the separator and the "this one" marker, in the accent, Sora 200
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>favicon</h3>
        <p style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;align-items:center">
          <img src="/favicon.svg" width="48" height="48" alt="">
          <img src="/favicon.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="">
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          before → after: the long grey bar, the short purple one. In the home
          window it is drawn with its real geometry and as tall as the rows
          beside it (both decided by Colin). Using it the same way wherever else
          a mark is needed is inferred
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>status dot</h3>
        <p><span class="dot" style="background:var(--ok9)"></span>live</p>
        <p class="f">
          with its word, never alone. None is in the site's chrome today: the
          home lab's stacks had one each until Nov 2026, and the home window
          says it in words (all up · live, 2 min ago). The status colours show
          only in post figures
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card mo">
        <h3>motion · hover to play</h3>
        {[
          ["fast", "hover, colour"],
          ["base", "a state change"],
          ["slow", "anything arriving"],
          ["draw", "bars growing"],
          ["line", "a line drawing"],
        ].map(([k, job]) => (
          <p class="mo__row">
            <span class="t-mono">{k}</span>
            <i style={`--d: var(--dur-${k})`}></i>
            <span class="f">{job}</span>
          </p>
        ))}
        <p class="f">
          curves: --ease for colour, --ease-out into place, --ease-in-out for a
          swap; rows follow by --stagger (70ms). One thing keeps moving on the
          first screen (the window's opening round plays once and rests); hover
          pauses; reduced motion stills everything
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Home window</h2>
    <p>
      The homepage's set piece: one terminal app, <code>~/ColinOS</code>, made
      of hairline panels. Outside code blocks it is the one place the site is
      framed, dense and coloured beyond the accent, and none of it travels to
      another page. It is made of six things: one tone, the hairline panel, a
      title in the border, a selected band, a readout and one focus. Which of
      its rules Colin decided and which are inferred from the version he picked
      is in DESIGN.md, How the home page grows. The sample is drawn from the
      real posts.
    </p>
    <div class="hw">
      <div class="hw__pn hw__pn--list is-focus">
        <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--t">writing</span>
        <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--tr">all {posts.length} posts</span>
        {rows.map((p, i) => (
          <p class:list={["hw__row", { "is-sel": i === 0 }]}>
            <span class="hw__date">{p.date}</span>
            <span class="hw__num">{p.minutes} min</span>
            <span class="hw__title">{p.title}</span>
            {/* biome-ignore format: a line break would show as a space beside the value */}
            <span>{p.before && <span class="f">{p.before} <StatArrow /></span>}{p.after && <b>{p.after}</b>}</span>
          </p>
        ))}
        {first && (
          <div class="hw__pv">
            <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--t hw__path">{first.slug}</span>
            {/* biome-ignore format: the same */}
            <p class="hw__stat">{first.before && <span class="f">{first.before} <StatArrow /></span>}{first.after}</p>
            <p class="hw__cap">
              {/* biome-ignore format: no space around the arrow but its own margin */}
              <span class="m">{first.label.split("→").map((part, k) => (<>{k > 0 && <StatArrow />}{part.trim()}</>))}</span>
              <span class="f">
                {first.date} {first.year} · {first.minutes} min read
              </span>
            </p>
          </div>
        )}
        <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--bl">j k select · enter open</span>
        <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--br">1 of {posts.length}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw__pn hw__pn--who">
        <span class="hw__lbl hw__lbl--t">fastfetch</span>
        <div class="hw__ff">
          <svg
            class="hw__mark"
            viewBox="0 0 128 128"
            role="img"
            aria-label="The favicon"
          >
            <rect class="is-tile" width="128" height="128" rx="24"></rect>
            <rect
              class="is-was"
              x="26"
              y="40"
              width="76"
              height="18"
              rx="5"
            ></rect>
            <rect
              class="is-now"
              x="26"
              y="70"
              width="30"
              height="18"
              rx="5"
            ></rect>
          </svg>
          <dl class="hw__kv">
            <div class="is-name">
              <dt>name</dt>
              <dd>
                <span>Colin Cheung</span>
                <span class="hw__cjk" lang="zh-Hant">張志權</span>
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="is-role">
              <dt>role</dt>
              <dd>Senior Software Engineer</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>work</dt>
              <dd>since {since}</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>posts</dt>
              <dd>
                {posts.length}
                {first && <span class="f hw__dim">· newest {first.date}</span>}
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid hw-notes">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>the panel</h3>
        <p>
          A 1px frame (ink at 17%), 6px corners, no fill. The title sits in the
          top border; the other corners take a readout or control, the key
          hints, a position or a link.
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          the only bordered panels on the site; a new one is a Panel in the
          window's grid, never a card
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>the focus</h3>
        <p>
          One panel at a time takes the accent on its border and title. It
          follows the pointer, the keyboard, or the scroll on a phone.
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          it moves, it never multiplies, and results inside stay ink. The
          window's other purple is the favicon's short bar, as in the browser
          tab; with the hero's highlight that is three on the first screen, the
          one exception to one purple per glance
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>the selected band · the readout</h3>
        <p>
          The chosen row of a list sits on ink at 6%, 9% while its panel has the
          focus. Under the list, ruled off by the hairline, a readout says its
          result large in Sora beside its label, with a path in the rule.
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          one band and one readout a list. The result is ink, never purple; a
          number still waiting is a thin faint dash
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>small print · four hues</h3>
        <p class="t-mono"><span class="hw__key">name role work</span> · keys</p>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <span class="hw__date">10 Nov · 06 12 18</span>
          · dates, time axes
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <span class="hw__num">5 min · 237 · 11°</span>
          · quantities (a read time, a count, an axis tick)
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <span class="hw__path">anki-listening-cards</span>
          · paths
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          the code theme's hues, as a highlighter would use them: a hue says
          what kind of thing a small datum is, never good or bad. Never a title,
          a result or a fill; nowhere but the window
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>live numbers</h3>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <b>237</b> <span class="f">live, 2 min ago</span>
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <b>237</b> <span class="f">last seen 3 h ago</span>
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono"><span class="f">– offline</span></p>
        <p class="t-mono"><span class="f">served from</span> London</p>
        <p class="t-mono"><span class="f">radar simulated here</span></p>
        <p class="t-mono">
          <span class="f">reward over time, sketched</span>
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          real or a dash, with its age in words. Anything drawn is true, or
          labelled as a sketch or a simulation. No stand-in numbers, no dot. A
          list from the server (the home lab's stacks, four chips then +N) is
          drawn at build and again when it lands, with no age: it says what is
          deployed, not what is up
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>how it grows</h3>
        <p>
          Decided by Colin: one window, not two; no swatches in fastfetch; the
          favicon drawn as itself and as tall as its rows; a stack scrolled
          through on a phone, one figure at a time on a desktop (the picker, Oct
          2026).
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          inferred from the version he picked, so ask before treating it as
          settled: new home-page content is a panel of this window or a figure
          in its picker, not a new section under it
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Posts</h2>
    <p>
      Posts are most of the site, so they get the same rules plus a kit for
      figures. One column for everything: text, figures, code and tables share
      one measure (44rem) and one left edge; wider breakout figures were tried
      and dropped. Real posts reskinned in this system:
      <code>shots/42-*.png</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>layout</h3>
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 220 120"
          style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block"
          aria-label="the post column"
        >
          <rect
            x="0"
            y="0"
            width="220"
            height="120"
            rx="6"
            fill="var(--well)"
          />
          <rect x="45" y="10" width="130" height="8" rx="2" fill="var(--n8)" />
          <rect
            x="45"
            y="24"
            width="60"
            height="6"
            rx="2"
            fill="var(--accent-solid)"
          />
          <rect x="45" y="38" width="130" height="3" fill="var(--n7)" />
          <rect x="45" y="45" width="120" height="3" fill="var(--n7)" />
          <rect
            x="45"
            y="56"
            width="130"
            height="30"
            rx="3"
            fill="var(--panel)"
          />
          <rect x="45" y="94" width="130" height="3" fill="var(--n7)" />
          <rect x="45" y="101" width="110" height="3" fill="var(--n7)" />
          <path d="M45 114H175" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-dasharray="2 2" />
          <text
            x="110"
            y="118"
            font-size="6"
            text-anchor="middle"
            fill="var(--faint)"
            style="font-family: var(--mono)"
          >
            44rem, one edge
          </text>
        </svg>
        <p class="f" style="margin-top:.6rem">
          a wide screen grows the type a little, never the line length; a
          reading-progress bar in the accent sits at the top
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>header</h3>
        <p class="t-mono" style="color:var(--faint)">// writing</p>
        <p
          style="font-family:var(--head);font-weight:600;font-size:1.35rem;letter-spacing:-.04em;line-height:1.1;margin:.2rem 0 .7rem"
        >
          CI faster than GitHub's, for free
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono" style="margin:0">vs GitHub</p>
        <p
          style="font-family:var(--head);font-weight:600;font-size:1.6rem;letter-spacing:-.04em;margin:0"
        >
          <span style="color:var(--faint);font-weight:400">26 s</span>
          <span class="f"><StatArrow /></span>
          <span style="color:var(--accent)">20 s</span>
        </p>
        <p class="t-mono">Oct 16, 2026 · 4 min read</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>prose</h3>
        <p style="font-size:1.0625rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0 0 .5rem">
          Geist 400 at 16–17px, line-height 1.65.
          <b style="font-weight:600">Bold</b>
          is 600.
          <a href="/blog/">Links</a>
          are the accent with a faint underline.
        </p>
        <p class="f" style="margin:0">
          each
          <span style="font-family:var(--head);font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)"
            >##</span
          >
          heading is Sora 600 with a hairline above it, so a long post scans as
          sections
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem">figure anatomy</h3>
    <figure
      class="card"
      style="margin:0;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted)"
    >
